+
+\subsection{Evolution Trace and Locked Holdout Endpoint}
+The most important EI audit artifact is the search trace, not only the final score. The advanced lane writes a candidate-order trace for every train/CV candidate evaluation, including the candidate family, head, calibrator, feature or component count, train/CV objective, train/CV AP, train/CV ROC AUC, and the running-best candidate at that point. Figure~\ref{fig:eievolution} uses that trace as an internal evolution plot and deliberately keeps the blind holdout out of the search curve.
+
+This separation is essential. A curve of holdout scores across candidate attempts would invite holdout shopping unless each point came from a separately pre-declared lock. EchoForge therefore reports the search trajectory as train/CV evidence and shows the holdout only once, as the endpoint of the written \texttt{selection\_lock.json}. In strict figure-generation mode, a leaderboard-only fallback is rejected because rank order is not the same as incremental evolution.
+
+\begin{figure*}[t]
+  \centering
+  \includegraphics[width=0.98\textwidth]{ei_evolution_money_plot.pdf}
+  \caption{EI evolution money plot. Left: internal train/CV candidate evaluations in actual evaluation order with the running best shown as a bold line. Middle: best train/CV AP reached by each search stage. Right: the single locked holdout endpoint after the selection lock. The holdout is not used to draw the evolution curve.}
+  \label{fig:eievolution}
+\end{figure*}
+
+\begin{table*}[t]
+  \caption{EI Evolution Trace Audit Contract}
+  \label{tab:eievolutioncontract}
+  \centering
+  \small
+  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.18\textwidth}p{0.30\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.20\textwidth}@{}}
+    \toprule
+    Artifact & Required fields & Reviewer check & Failure mode guarded \\
+    \midrule
+    \texttt{evolution\_trace.csv} & candidate order, stage, family, calibrator, train/CV objective, train/CV AP, train/CV ROC AUC, running best & curve is an actual search trace rather than a sorted leaderboard & fake evolution plot \\
+    \texttt{selection\_lock.json} & selected candidate, calibrator, component IDs, train/CV rank, split-isolation policy & selected point matches the trace and is frozen before holdout & silent reselection \\
+    \texttt{ei\_evolution\_summary.json} & trace basis, true-trace flag, selected row, holdout endpoint & paper can fail strict mode when only rank fallback exists & rank-order masquerading as evolution \\
+    Holdout endpoint & AP, ROC AUC, FPR, F1, gate status for the locked candidate only & holdout is shown once after lock & holdout tuning \\
+    \bottomrule
+  \end{tabular}
+\end{table*}
 
 This distinction matters because a post-hoc view control can be strong on one metric without being the final EI artifact. In this run, the passive-RF-only view has higher AP and swept Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR than the full EI candidate, which is an important diagnostic red flag rather than a contradiction to hide. Its selected-threshold F1 and ECE are worse, and it does not carry the same calibrated-fusion selection lock. We therefore report EI as the pre-specified, calibrated selected-threshold artifact, not as a universal dominator of every view control.
